    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Deformation bands in porous sandstones have a broad spectrum of microstructures with two end members: they may display cataclasis and porosity reduction or they may show positive dilatancy with little or no cataclasis. Factors controlling the development of a particular microstructure include clay-content, porosity of the host rock, and the stress state at the time of deformation. The petrophysical properties of deformation bands are controlled by their microstructure. Permeability measurements performed with a minipermeameter show that permeability perpendicular to a deformation band can drop 1 to 4 orders of magnitude with respect to that of the host rock. The reduction in permeability perpendicular to faults is more pronounced- more than 7 orders of magnitude less than in the surrounding host rock - when slip is localized on a plane next to a zone of deformation bands.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
